From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] phy: allwinner: A733 SERDES combo-PHY -- port the branch the vendor takes

The PMA config this driver programmed was a faithful port of the wrong half of
an if statement, so USB3 never left CSFTRST: LINK_STUS0 stayed 0x001 for the
full 500 ms poll -- CLK_EN_ACK (bit4) and POWER_STATE_ACK (bits [13:8]) never
set although the common PLL had locked (PMA_CMN_READY = 1) -- and dwc3 then
died with -110.

combo0_usb_param_config() forks immediately:

	if (sunxi_cphy->udp_supported) {
		combo0_configure_usb_dp(combo0);
		return;
	}

udp_supported is !device_property_read_bool(dev, "udp-disable"), and
"udp-disable" appears nowhere in the vendor device trees, so on this SoC the
vendor always takes combo0_configure_usb_dp() and returns. Our table was ported
from the code below that return -- 169 writes that never execute on this part.

The two branches differ in 58 PMA registers (42 the vendor writes and we did
not, 15 written with a different value, 1 we wrote and it does not). Deciding
between them from the vendor source alone would be exactly the mistake that got
us here, so the branch was settled against silicon instead: the 8 GiB board's vendor
capture taken with SuperSpeed linked at 10 Gbps covers 30 of those 58 registers.

	silicon agrees with the udp branch : 29
	silicon agrees with the old table  :  0
	neither                            :  1  (0x01C1, changed by training)
	outside the captured window        : 28

That window gap is now closed on a second board. The 6 GiB board runs 6.18.36-edge with the
BSP combophy and a live SuperSpeed Plus Gen 2x1 link, so the whole 128 KiB
aperture could be read (python3 mmap on /dev/mem; read() gives EFAULT on arm64,
STRICT_DEVMEM is off).
All 58 registers are readable there:

	silicon agrees with the udp branch : 54
	silicon agrees with the old table  :  0
	neither                            :  4
	unreadable                         :  0

of which the previously-unreachable upper half alone is 25 / 0 / 3. Two boards,
two kernel vintages, both linked, both against the table this patch removes.

The 4 "neither" are 0x01C1, 0xC011, 0xE003 and 0xE005: they match neither table,
i.e. hardware or link training rewrites them, so they are not config constants.
0xE003 is worth naming -- we wrote 0x0001, the vendor writes it on neither path,
and silicon reads 0x001B.

Control for the decode: over the 49 registers where both branches agree, the
same address mapping reproduces silicon 49/49, so the idx<<1 / 16-bit-in-32-bit
read-back is validated rather than assumed.

So a733_pma_cfg[] is regenerated from combo0_configure_usb_dp() (orientation
NORMAL, ssc_en = 0 -- the vendor never parses ssc_en, its DT hook is commented
out): 231 writes in three blocks, split by the two non-PMA writes the vendor
performs in the middle of the sequence. That ordering is kept, not normalised:
LINK_CTRL1 is written as an absolute 0x01100001 after the CMN/lane-0-1 block,
and TYPEC_CONN_DIR is cleared before the lane-2/3 block rather than at the end.
0xE003 = 0x0001 is dropped (the udp path never writes it; upstream
phy-cadence-torrent.c only touches PHY_PMA_PLL_RAW_CTRL on the DP rate-change
path, and raw-forcing PLL0 while the link expects sideband control is a
plausible way to get CMN_READY without CLK_EN_ACK -- untested either way).

Second change, same root: the bring-up moves out of probe() into phy_ops.init.
dwc3 calls phy_init() four lines before its own soft reset (dwc3_phy_setup ->
dwc3_phy_init -> dwc3_core_soft_reset), i.e. after it has deasserted RST_USB_2
and written GUSB3PIPECTL. Doing the PIPE handshake in probe means dwc3 re-toggles
its side afterwards and nothing re-runs it. probe() does supplies and the
RST_BUS_SERDES deassert and nothing that touches the link; phy_ops.init does the
rest and phy_ops.exit puts the PHY back in IDDQ. This split alone was HW-tested
earlier in a test build and was necessary but not sufficient -- with the wrong table
it could not be anything else.

Third change, from 2026-07-29: the SERDES subsystem page at 0x06C00000 is not
this driver's. Its two USB3 bus gates and the companion-PHY reset-n were written
from here, which is what made the dwc3 core's ability to identify itself depend
on this driver probing first -- an ordering fw_devlink stopped honouring once the
Type-C connector's graph closed a ring. They are a clock and a reset and they
belong to the provider added in `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-serdes-subsystem-clocks` (gates) and to the companion PHY driver
`phy-allwinner-a733-add-usb2-companion-phy` (its own reset). Three consequences here: reg window 0 is gone, so the
windows are re-indexed -- link first, because a node's name has to be its first
reg and the link block is what this PHY is; and the block reset becomes shared,
because that provider holds it deasserted and asserting it from here would take
its registers away. Dropping the window also ends this driver's exclusive claim
on 4 KiB it shares with the PCIe bus gates at 0x0004 -- those read
0x00070000 on the 6 GiB board, i.e. in use. And third, the shared deassert is now balanced by a
devres action, so an unbind gives the line back instead of pinning it deasserted
forever -- the previous code only asserted it on a probe error path.

Checked against the A733 user manual, not just the vendor tree:
  - LINK_CTRL0..3 / LINK_STUS0 offsets and bit meanings are anchored to
    the A733 user manual; the absolute 0x01100001 is
    the value read live on the 8 GiB board with SS up.
  - Two offsets this driver writes had no documentation anchor when this was
    written and are marked open rather than implied covered:
    USB3P1_PIPE_RXD_MAP_CTRL (top2 + 0x0104; only its 0x0100 CLK_MAP twin was
    anchored) and DP_PHY_CFG_DATA_EN, bit0 of DP_RAWS_TXDW_CONV_CTRL (only
    [11:8] and [7:4] were anchored). Both come from the vendor sequence and are
    unverified; `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence` later anchors
    bit0 as TX_PHY_CFG_DATA_EN (UM V0.92 18.2.6.24).
  - the PMA body itself is NOT covered by any register documentation
    available to this work. Every PMA claim here
    therefore rests on the silicon capture plus upstream phy-cadence-torrent.c
    block geometry, and is labelled as such. No manual anchor is
    implied where none exists.
  - window bounds: the highest index written is 0xE006 -> byte 0x1C00C, which is
    inside COMBPHY0's aperture 0x06C80000..0x06C9FFFF.
    That entry's "64K" size cell contradicts its own range -- an inconsistency
    in the UM's memory map (the same holds for COMBPHY1/GMAC0/PCIE0).
    We follow the range, as does the vendor DT;
    both it and our `arm64-dts-a733-add-usb3-dwc3-combophy` map 0x20000. Not silicon-checked above 0x06C8FFFF yet.

Board scope, since a vendor driver serves more than one board: the udp path is
not another board's configuration. This board's own vendor DTS puts DP-alt and
USB3 on the same Type-C (combo0_dp + combo0_usb, extcon on &serdes); USB-C J4
carries DP-alt and SuperSpeed, mutually exclusive. The board-specific arrays
lane_invert/lane_remap/typec_remap do exist in that DTS, but combo0_configure_
usb_dp() (1892-2311) never references them -- they are consumed by the DP-side
functions -- so nothing board-specific was dropped from what is ported here.
What IS a limitation: orientation is hard-coded NORMAL. The vendor picks the
REVERSE variant from TCPC input, which we have no source for, so a flipped
Type-C plug may not train until that is wired up.

Honest limits: matching a working board's end state is a witness argument, not a
mechanism proof -- it says what a linked PHY looks like, not which write is the
gate. Read-back also cannot see a write whose value equals the reset default, and
the 4 hardware-rewritten registers above are invisible to this method by
construction. The reading itself is now first-hand and complete, but "this is the
config a 10 Gbps link runs" is not the same claim as "this makes our link train".

HW-pending, single observable: on the 8 GiB board, LINK_STUS0 @0x06C01900 must go from
0x001 to bit4 and bits[13:8] set inside the existing 500 ms poll, CSFTRST must
clear, and dwc3 must probe without -110.

Based on drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c from radxa/allwinner-bsp
(commit b36af3872d4e, "aiot-linux-v1.4.6 release"; the file is unchanged on
the branches cubie-aiot-v1.4.6 and cubie-aiot-v1.4.8), GPL-2.0,
Copyright(c) 2020 - 2023 Allwinner Technology Co.,Ltd. and
Copyright (C) 2024 Allwinner Electronics Co., Ltd. The PMA table is ported
from its combo0_configure_usb_dp(), the init sequence from
combo0_usb_phy_init() and combo0_usb_phy_reset():
https://github.com/radxa/allwinner-bsp/blob/b36af3872d4e6e91da6e929f4ccf4822b3ea9d66/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c

